A: If you're creating unit tests in Visual Studio, you can use Microsoft Fakes Framework and Visual Studio QA tools to create unit tests without writing any code. 

Microsoft Fakes Framework allows you to create, manage, and modify the runtime behavior of .NET Framework types in unit tests. 
Visual Studio QA tools helps to write, manage, and run unit tests, test methods, and test lists for .NET Framework applications. 

I don't see any issues to do with your import statements, so I recommend you to check these two before you test the 'PexAssumeUnderTest' and other attributes again.

Hope this helps, let me know if you have any further issues.

A: The error message suggests that the "PexAssumeUnderTest" attribute is not recognized. 

This is probably because the NuGet package containing this attribute is not installed. 

In order to install the Microsoft Pex package, you should follow these steps:

1. In Solution Explorer right-click the test project and select Manage NuGet Packages
2. Search and install the Microsoft.Pex package.

Hopefully, you can now use PexAssumeUnderTest and the other Pex attributes in your test project without any problem.

If this still does not solve the problem, it could be a problem with the  version of .NET framework you are using, as the Pex attributes might not be present in a version of the .NET framework this high. In this case, you will need to use a lower version.
To confirm if this is the issue, you could try running the following:
