--------------------            if (New_Ret == Old_arrListCount - 1)
                throw new Exception();

            if (New_Ret == -1 && Old_arrListCount != 0)
                throw new Exception();

            // if the x has never been added into the arraylist , lastIndexOf should return -1
            if (Old_arrListContainsX == false && New_Ret != -1)
                throw new Exception();

            // the result should not exceed the size of the list
            if (New_Ret >= New_arrListCount)
                throw new Exception();

            // the last index of x should be equal  to the size -1 if x is in the list
            if (New_arrListContainsX == true && New_Ret != New_arrListCount - 1)
                throw new Exception();

            // the last IndexOf return should never be greater than the size of arraylist
            if (New_Ret > New_arrListCount)
                throw new Exception();
        }
    }
}

```
The above test method verifies the contract of the LastIndexOf method under different conditions and throws an exception whenever it finds a misbehavior of the LastIndexOf method. PexObserve.ValueForViewing is used to output the values of some key variables, which helps understand the state of the list and the result more intuitively. 

PexAssumeUnderTest attribute is used to specify the target method in our case, LastIndexOf, which is under test. 

If the contract of the LastIndexOf method is not broken by the test, the program won’t throw any exceptions, and the test passes. If there is a bug in the LastIndexOf method, the test will fail with an exception. The test verifies that the LastIndexOf method follows its contract, where it should return the last occurrence of the given object in this ArrayList, or -1 if not found. 

The ArrayList class in .NET provides a great deal of functionality for working with lists, but testing such functionality manually can be tedious. This is when automated testing frameworks like Pex (Pathfinder) come in handy. Pex can help test many interesting and challenging aspects of real-world code paths, discover bugs, and ensure that your code meets its design and functionality requirements.

The Pex tool can be used to perform the following tasks:

1. Generate test input data that you can use for testing your class methods
2. Execute your class methods with different input values to check how they handle different situations
3. Identify branches, method calls, and variables with incorrect behavior
4. Generate code that validates all possible paths through your classes and methods

Pex can greatly speed up the development process by providing the missing input data, letting you explore different program execution paths, and identifying design and implementation issues. 

In testing, it is important to conduct tests for all possible conditions and edge cases, and also the class/method contract. For collections, the tests should cover all the most important operations: adding an element, removing an element, searching for an element, iterating, etc. Additionally, it is essential to test the functionality when there are no elements, when there is one element, and when the capacity limit is approached. 

Pex can definitely augment your test coverage, but how effective will be highly dependent on how well it can understand the rules and regulations of the code being tested and its context. Its utility should be considered based on these factors to ensure it provides the desired results.

