Please note that the code is actually not a real test, but it's just for illustrating the concepts. Pex does not support method contracts or real testing scenarios. The PUT_LastIndexOfContract method is an example of what the contract test could look like, it is not a real test and won't pass. For a real test, you should write a proper test method to cover all the possible cases. For more information about how to test an ArrayList, you might need to look at ArrayList documentation and the code.

In this example if the return value is negative, it would fail the assertion in the PUT_LastIndexOfContract method. The PexAssert.IsFalse(New_Ret < 0); makes sure this is the case.

Pex will generate a set of tests based on your method parameters. In this case, if your ArrayList has elements, and you insert a new item, it would generate a set of tests to see if the LastIndexOf method works as expected. However, in reality, you can't predict the outcome of LastIndexOf method, since it simply returns the last index of the provided item in the ArrayList.

Please keep in mind that Pex is intended for unit testing and it primarily operates at the method level, not the class or collection levels as ArrayList does. Also, you may need to understand the ArrayLists' behavior for a more complete test suite.

Remember that Pex is primarily designed for parameter randomization, and will not generate comprehensive tests for corner cases or failure conditions. Pex operates best where methods have well-defined contracts and throw exceptions for misuse, as it relies on these to generate meaningful tests. For more detailed or complex test scenarios, you should consider using other tools or methods. This also includes NUnit testing library to utilize its powerful features like attribute-based test methods or test [theories]. Also, if you want to test the ArrayList class, then I would recommend testing it from a higher service layer by using MOQ or NSubstitute frameworks. That would give you more control on the behavior of your class being tested.

Hope this helps and best of luck with your testing!!

PS: Please note that the code I provided is actually more of a theory than a practical implementation. Practical unit testing in C# will use real unit testing libraries/frameworks (like NUnit or XUnit), not Pex. Pex is a powerful tool but it is targeted towards automated unit testing and as such the ability to execute is not guaranteed. Also, not all unit tests are suitable to be automated. Some tests may be too dependent on specific environment variables or other resources and can't or shouldn't be automated.

Please keep these points in mind while using Pex for testing. If you want to see how Pex works, you may want to explore the testing tutorials available on Pex's official website. Also, take a look at Automated Unit Testing with NUnit in Visual Studio.NET by Joe Mayo to know more about creating and running test cases with NUnit.--------------------            if (New_Ret >=0 )
