```

This is the Pex method that was added for the contract test of the LastIndexOf method from the ArrayList class. 

This method will run a series of assertions on all key properties within the ArrayList object. The properties observed are:

1. The old and new counts of the ArrayList object
2. The previous index of the integer x within the array list
3. The presence (or absence) of the integer x in the array list.

Then, the method checks if the LastIndexOf method's returned value is negative. If it is, it asserts if the old count is not -1.

Finally, if the assumptions hold and the method doesn't fail any assertions, the test passes.

This way, Pex can verify the behavior of the LastIndexOf method for different scenarios. 

Remember, Pex can't verify that LastIndexOf returns the actual index for an existing x's last occurrence in the list; it verifies only that it's equal to or greater than 0 for existing xs. Pex can only provide input for which it detects that the program's behavior might change under specific conditions.

Please note that an exception isn't thrown when the parameter x doesn't exist in the list. The IndexOf method will return -1 in this situation. To see that an exception might be thrown in this situation, one should use a more complex sequence of operations that includes adding elements and then removing them in a way that makes the list state resemble what it will be after removal of x.

Further, the tests are conducted under the assumption that the method to be tested has not been changed since the last time it passed the tests. If this is not the case, then the tests need to be updated accordingly.

[PexMethodUnderTest] attribute isn't needed here because the method, to be tested, is not part of the class.

The PexClass attribute also serves no role in this particular scenario, since the class does not contain any instance data to be tested. Also, PexClass attribute is being used too late in this scenario. It should be used as soon as possible, before any test method gets generated. It is not a problem if the class or the method is not yet complete. Pex generates tests even in an incomplete code. However, it needs to have visibility on the full code structure and behavior in order to generate meaningful tests.

```C#
    [TestClass]
    public class ArrayListTestClass
    {
        [PexMethodUnderTest]
        [TestMethod]
        public void TestLastIndexOf()
        {
            ArrayListContractTest t = new ArrayListContractTest();
            t.PUT_LastIndexOfContract(null/*To be filled*/, 0/*To be filled*/);
        }
    }

```
Manual filling is required for each test case, where 'null' and '0' are place-holders for actual parameters to be tested.

The above mentioned is the complete process to conduct a contract test for the LastIndexOf method for the ArrayList class. This ensures a method's behavior remains consistent after modifications in the code.

This test does not check whether the LastIndexOf method finds the most recent occurrence of an item; rather it checks whether it finds any instance at all. This is due to the properties and test requirements of the LastIndexOf method and how it may or may not function based on the ArrayList's own status and its input parameters.

Keep in mind that the ArrayList class has more than just the LastIndexOf method, so adding tests for all of its method would enhance the coverage, and aid in the detection of potential flaws. Also, always remember to check if the method is still necessary and up-to-date with new updates in the base class or .NET Framework.

Keep in mind that due to the fact that LastIndexOf method returns -1 if the element is not found, you can't use it as a contract test. PexContractTest requires parameters that can be tested for different outcomes, so you should avoid using methods like LastIndexOf(), when they can be easily implemented using other methods or proper checks.

Lastly, if the LastIndexOf() method might fail in some scenarios, consider adding separate tests with different parameters where LastIndexOf might have different output values.

In summary, a contract test should be performed for methods whenever possible. In cases where the method's behavior may vary across different--------------------            PexAssert.AreNotEqual(-1, Old_arrListCount, "$old_arrList_Count");

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ If the return value is non-negative, new list size is not -1
            PexAssert.AreNotEqual(-1, New_arrListCount, "$new_arrList_Count");
 
            // Post conditions 
            // Assert.AreEqual\(Old_arrListIndexOfX, New_arrListIndexOfX, "The LastIndexOf method should not change the index of an existing element in the list");
	    // Assert.AreEqual\(Old_arrListContainsX, New_arrListContainsX, "The LastIndexOf method should not affect the Contains method");
	    // Assert.AreEqual\(Old_x, x, "The argument of the LastIndexOf method should not change before the return value");
	}
    }
}
```

