}

This example test PUT contract of LastIndexOf method.

AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true); - This line is not actually needed, but is useful for illustratively showing the purpose of this line in a test. If the condition in this line is false, then the remainder of the test will not execute.

// an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ If the return value is negative, old list size is not -1.
Debug.Assert(New_Ret != -1 && New_arrListCount != -1 && Old_arrListContainsX); - This line of code makes use of the Debug.Assert function to assert that the conditions specified are true. If the conditions aren't true, then Debug.Assert will throw an exception, which will cause the test to fail.

This test demonstrates all of the usual considerations that are important for testing: assumption, preparation, invocation of the method under test, observation, and assertion.

In conclusion, PexObserve.ValueForViewing, Debug.Assert, and AssumePrecondition will all be essential tools in a tester's kit.

Note: Keep in mind that while this test meets the requirements for a Pex unit test, it is not a complete test suite. Depending on your need, you'll likely need additional tests to have a comprehensive suite.
  
  [Back to top](#top)**Adding multiple values**In the Pex unit test for a List, we're only inserting a single integer into the List. This makes it rather pointless, so it's generally recommended to create methods that add multiple values at once, if possible. This allows for more meaningful testing scenarios, as it covers more edge cases.In the given scenario, we are testing the insert method which has a signature:
      
      void Insert(int index, object value)
    
As we know Insert() method will add an item into the ArrayList at a specific location, our unit test might look like

```csharp
[TestMethod]
[HostType("Moles")]
public void TestInsertPositive()
{
    // Arrange
    ArrayList al = new ArrayList();
    object obj = new object();
    al.Add(1);
    al.Add(2);
    al.Add(3);
    int oldCount = al.Count;

    // Act
    al.Insert(1, obj);
    int newCount = al.Count;

    // Assert
    Assert.AreEqual(oldCount + 1, newCount);
    Assert.AreEqual(obj, al[1]);
}
```

In this unit test, we insert an object at the second index of the ArrayList. We store the count of elements before and after the insert operation. If the insertion is successful, the count of elements after insert operation should be one more than the before operation and the inserted object should at the index where it was supposed to be inserted.

[Back to top](#top)**Removing multiple values**When removing items from ArrayList, ArrayList has method RemoveRange() which removes all the elements starting from the specified index to the end of the ArrayList. This makes it rather pointless to just test Remove() method without removing multiple values at the same time.

In the given scenario, we are testing the RemoveRange() method. The method has the following signature:

    void RemoveRange(int index, int count);

As per the scenario, we need to test the removal of multiple values at a specific index. The unit test might look like:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
[HostType("Moles")]
public void TestRemoveRangePositive()
{
    // Arrange
    var al = new ArrayList();
    for(int i = 1; i <= 10; i++)
    {
        al.Add(i);
    }
    int oldCount = al.Count;

    // Act
    al.RemoveRange(2,5);
    int newCount = al.Count;

    // Assert
    Assert.AreEqual(oldCount - 5, newCount);
    for(int i = 0; i < 5; i++)
    {
        Assert.IsNull(al[2 + i]);
    }
}
```
In this unit test, we remove 5 items starting from the index 2. We store the count of elements before and after the removal operation. If the removal is--------------------